(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-female-reproductive-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Female Reproductive System — Anatomy Exam 3 (Set 2)","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"The paired embryonic ducts that develop into the major internal female reproductive organs are the:","choices":["Paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts","Mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts","Pronephric embryonic ducts","Metanephric collecting ducts"],"answer":0,"exp":"The paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts develop into the female internal reproductive organs (uterine tubes, uterus, cervix, upper vagina) in the absence of anti-Müllerian hormone. — Slide 16 (The Müllerian ducts)"},{"q":"In the female embryo, the fused caudal portions of the Müllerian ducts form the uterus, cervix, and:","choices":["Lower two-thirds of the vagina","Upper (upper one-third) vagina","Fallopian (uterine) tubes","Ovarian cortex and medulla"],"answer":1,"exp":"The fused caudal portions of the Müllerian ducts form the uterus, cervix, and the upper portion (upper one-third to two-thirds) of the vagina. — Slide 16 (Müllerian ducts differentiation)"},{"q":"The unfused cranial portions of the Müllerian ducts differentiate into the:","choices":["Uterus, cervix, and fundus","Fallopian (uterine) tubes","Lower one-third of vagina","Labia majora skin folds"],"answer":1,"exp":"The unfused cranial portions of the Müllerian ducts differentiate into the fallopian (uterine) tubes. — Slide 16 (Müllerian ducts differentiation)"},{"q":"In males, the Müllerian ducts regress due to the action of:","choices":["Anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH)","Testosterone from the testes","Estradiol from Sertoli cells","Inhibin from Sertoli cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH), secreted by fetal Sertoli cells, causes regression of the Müllerian ducts in males. — Slides 16–17 (Müllerian system)"},{"q":"The lower portion of the vagina develops embryologically from the:","choices":["Fused Müllerian ducts","Gubernaculum","Mesonephric ducts","Urogenital sinus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lower portion of the vagina develops from the urogenital sinus. — Slide 16 (Müllerian ducts)"},{"q":"The Müllerian ducts arise from the coelomic epithelium around which week of development?","choices":["2nd week","20th week","12th week","6th week"],"answer":3,"exp":"The paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts arise from the coelomic epithelium lateral to the mesonephros around the 6th week of development, fusing caudally by about the 12th week. — Slides 16–17 (Müllerian development)"},{"q":"The perineum is a diamond-shaped area bounded anteriorly by the pubic symphysis, posteriorly by the coccyx, and laterally by the:","choices":["Ischial tuberosities","Iliac crest borders","Greater trochanters","Sacral promontory point"],"answer":0,"exp":"The perineum is bounded anteriorly by the pubic symphysis, posteriorly by the coccyx, and laterally by the ischial tuberosities. — Slide 5 (Perineum)"},{"q":"An imaginary horizontal line between the ischial tuberosities divides the perineum into the anterior triangle and the:","choices":["Urogenital (anterior) triangle","Lumbar (paraspinal) triangle","Femoral (inguinal) triangle","Posterior (anal) triangle"],"answer":3,"exp":"The transverse line between the ischial tuberosities divides the perineum into the anterior urogenital triangle and the posterior (anal) triangle. — Slide 5 (Perineum)"},{"q":"In females, the anterior (urogenital) triangle contains the:","choices":["Urethral and vaginal orifices","The anus and anal sphincter","The ovaries and fallopian tubes","The uterine tubes and ovaries"],"answer":0,"exp":"In females, the anterior (urogenital) triangle contains the urethral and vaginal orifices. — Slide 5 (Perineum)"},{"q":"The posterior (anal) triangle of the perineum contains which structure in both sexes?","choices":["The clitoris","The anus","The vaginal orifice","The urethra"],"answer":1,"exp":"The posterior (anal) triangle contains the anus in both sexes. — Slide 5 (Perineum)"},{"q":"The peritoneal fold that drapes over the uterus and holds the ovaries and uterine tubes is the:","choices":["Round ligament","Broad ligament","Ovarian ligament","Suspensory ligament"],"answer":1,"exp":"The broad ligament is the peritoneal fold draping over the uterus that also helps hold the ovaries and uterine tubes in place. — Slides 14, 18 (Peritoneal Folds/Ligaments)"},{"q":"The double fold of peritoneum that attaches the ovaries to the broad ligament is the:","choices":["Mesovarium","Mesosalpinx","Mesometrium","Round ligament"],"answer":0,"exp":"The mesovarium is the double fold of peritoneum that attaches each ovary to the posterior layer of the broad ligament. — Slide 14 (Peritoneal Folds/Ligaments)"},{"q":"Which ligament connects the ovary to the uterus?","choices":["Suspensory ligament","Round ligament","Ovarian ligament","Broad ligament"],"answer":2,"exp":"The ovarian ligament attaches the ovary to the superior/lateral margin of the uterus. — Slides 14, 18 (ligaments)"},{"q":"The suspensory ligament of the ovary extends from the ovary/mesovarium to the:","choices":["Uterine fundus wall","Sacral promontory","Labia majora tissue","Pelvic (body) wall"],"answer":3,"exp":"The suspensory ligament of the ovary extends from the ovary and mesovarium to the pelvic (body) wall, carrying the ovarian vessels, nerves, and lymphatics. — Slides 14, 18 (ligaments)"},{"q":"The round ligament of the uterus travels from the uterus through the inguinal canal to end in the:","choices":["Labia majora","Sacral surface","Pelvic side wall","Ovarian hilum"],"answer":0,"exp":"The round ligament of the uterus travels through the inguinal canal and terminates in the connective tissue of the labia majora, helping maintain the uterus's anteverted position. — Slides 18, 36 (round ligament)"},{"q":"Which paired ligaments — thickenings of subserous fascia and smooth muscle — support the cervix and vagina laterally to the pelvic wall?","choices":["Transverse cervical (cardinal) ligaments","Uterosacral (rectouterine) ligaments","Round ligaments of the uterus","Broad ligaments of the uterus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The transverse cervical (cardinal) ligaments are thickenings of subserous fascia and smooth muscle that anchor the cervix and vagina laterally to the pelvic wall. — Slides 31, 36 (uterine support)"},{"q":"The uterosacral ligaments extend from the inferior uterus to which structure?","choices":["The pubic symphysis","The lateral pelvic wall","The labia majora","The sacrum"],"answer":3,"exp":"The uterosacral ligaments extend from the inferior uterus (cervix) posteriorly to the sacrum, helping support the uterus and hold the cervix in position. — Slide 36 (uterine support)"},{"q":"The pelvic diaphragm is formed by the coccygeus muscle and the:","choices":["Ischiocavernosus","Bulbospongiosus","Levator ani","Obturator internus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pelvic diaphragm is formed by the coccygeus and levator ani muscles, which together support the pelvic viscera and close the inferior pelvic outlet. — Slide 9 (Pelvic Diaphragm)"},{"q":"The levator ani muscle is composed of the puborectalis, iliococcygeus, and:","choices":["Pubococcygeus","Coccygeus","Bulbospongiosus","Piriformis"],"answer":0,"exp":"The levator ani is composed of the puborectalis, pubococcygeus, and iliococcygeus muscles. — Slide 9 (Pelvic Diaphragm)"},{"q":"A primary function of the pelvic diaphragm is to:","choices":["Produce ova through monthly ovarian follicular maturation","Secrete estrogen and progesterone into systemic circulation","Support the pelvic organs and seal the inferior opening of the bony pelvis","Transport sperm through the male reproductive tract ducts"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pelvic diaphragm supports the pelvic organs, seals the inferior opening of the bony pelvis, and lifts to assist defecation. — Slide 9 (Pelvic Diaphragm)"},{"q":"Which muscle, a part of the levator ani, is specifically related to the vagina?","choices":["Pubovaginalis","Coccygeus","Piriformis","Obturator internus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pubovaginalis, a component of the levator ani, is specifically related to the vagina. — Slide 62 (Muscles Related to the Vagina)"},{"q":"The ovarian artery, supplying the ovary, arises directly from the:","choices":["Internal iliac artery","Abdominal aorta","Uterine artery","External iliac artery"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ovarian artery arises directly from the abdominal aorta (just inferior to the renal arteries) and reaches the ovary via the suspensory ligament. — Slides 24, 27, 41 (ovarian vasculature)"},{"q":"The uterine artery, the main supply to the uterus, is a branch of the:","choices":["Abdominal aorta directly","Internal iliac artery","Ovarian artery branch","Femoral artery branch"],"answer":1,"exp":"The uterine artery, the main blood supply to the uterus, arises from the internal iliac artery. — Slides 35, 41 (uterine blood supply)"},{"q":"Venous drainage of the ovaries is asymmetric: the right ovarian vein drains to the IVC, while the left ovarian vein drains to the:","choices":["Left renal vein","Portal vein","Internal iliac vein","Azygos vein"],"answer":0,"exp":"The right ovarian vein drains directly into the inferior vena cava, while the left ovarian vein drains into the left renal vein. — Slide 24 (Ovary venous drainage)"},{"q":"The blood supply to the vagina is provided by the uterine, vaginal, and which additional arteries?","choices":["Femoral arteries","Ovarian arteries only","Pudendal arteries","Obturator arteries"],"answer":2,"exp":"The vagina is supplied by the uterine and vaginal arteries along with the (internal) pudendal arteries. — Slide 59 (Vagina blood supply)"},{"q":"The uterine tube (fallopian tube) receives its blood supply from the ovarian and which other arteries?","choices":["Inferior epigastric arteries","Pudendal arteries","Vaginal arteries","Uterine arteries"],"answer":3,"exp":"The uterine (fallopian) tube receives its blood supply from both the ovarian and uterine arteries. — Slide 48 (Uterine Tube blood supply)"},{"q":"The venous drainage of the ovary forms a network within the suspensory ligament called the:","choices":["Vesical venous plexus","Pampiniform plexus","Prostatic venous plexus","Vertebral venous plexus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Ovarian venous blood drains through a network within the suspensory ligament called the pampiniform plexus, analogous to the venous drainage of the testis in males. — Slide 27 (Vasculature of Ovaries)"},{"q":"The ovaries receive parasympathetic innervation from which nerves?","choices":["Pudendal nerve","Greater splanchnic nerves","Pelvic splanchnic nerves","Vagus nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"The ovaries receive parasympathetic innervation via the pelvic splanchnic nerves. — Slide 27 (Innervation of Ovaries)"},{"q":"Afferent (sensory) fibers from the ovary enter the spinal cord through which spinal nerve levels?","choices":["T5 and T6","T10 and T11","L1 and L2","S2 and S4"],"answer":1,"exp":"Afferent (sensory) fibers from the ovary enter the spinal cord at the T10 and T11 levels, which explains referred ovarian pain to the periumbilical region. — Slide 27 (Innervation of Ovaries)"},{"q":"The uterine tube receives its nerve supply from the ovarian and uterine:","choices":["Plexi","Splanchnic trunks","Pudendal branches","Sacral roots"],"answer":0,"exp":"The uterine tube receives its nerve supply from the ovarian and uterine (nerve) plexi. — Slide 48 (Uterine Tube nerve supply)"},{"q":"The external urethral sphincter and much of the perineum receive somatic innervation (voluntary control) from which nerve?","choices":["Obturator nerve","Pudendal nerve","Pelvic splanchnic nerve","Genitofemoral nerve"],"answer":1,"exp":"The pudendal nerve provides somatic (voluntary) innervation to the external urethral sphincter and much of the perineum. — Slides 62, 65 (perineal/vaginal innervation)"},{"q":"The uterus is located between which two organs?","choices":["The urinary bladder and the rectum","The ovaries and sigmoid colon","The vagina and the cervix","The kidneys and the ureters"],"answer":0,"exp":"The uterus is located between the urinary bladder anteriorly and the rectum posteriorly. — Slide 32 (Uterus)"},{"q":"Which sequence gives the regions of the uterus from superior to inferior?","choices":["Cervix → isthmus → body → fundus","Isthmus → fundus → body → cervix","Body → fundus → cervix → isthmus","Fundus → body → isthmus → cervix"],"answer":3,"exp":"From superior to inferior, the regions of the uterus are the fundus, body, isthmus, and cervix. — Slides 33–34 (Regions of the Uterus)"},{"q":"The normal uterus is described as anteverted and anteflexed; anteflexion refers to the bend at the junction of the:","choices":["Cervix and vagina","Isthmus and fundus","Fundus and body","Cervix and body"],"answer":3,"exp":"Anteflexion refers to the forward bend of the uterus at the junction of the cervix and body, while anteversion is the forward bend at the cervix-vagina junction. — Slide 31 (Uterus)"},{"q":"The cervix attaches to the vagina at approximately what angle, forming pockets called fornices?","choices":["45 degrees","180 degrees","90 degrees","30 degrees"],"answer":2,"exp":"The cervix attaches to the vagina at approximately a 90-degree angle, creating the recesses known as the fornices around the cervix. — Slide 60 (Cervix and Vagina)"},{"q":"The uterine tube's regions, from the ovary toward the uterus, are the infundibulum (with fimbriae), ampulla, isthmus, and:","choices":["Fundus region of the uterine body","Intramural (interstitial/uterine) segment","Cervix at the lower uterine segment","Fornix surrounding the cervix"],"answer":1,"exp":"The uterine tube's four regions, from the ovary toward the uterus, are the infundibulum, ampulla, isthmus, and intramural (interstitial/uterine) segment, which passes through the uterine wall. — Slides 45, 51 (Uterine Tube regions)"},{"q":"Fertilization of the secondary oocyte typically occurs in which part of the uterine tube?","choices":["Infundibulum","Intramural segment","Isthmus","Ampulla"],"answer":3,"exp":"Fertilization of the secondary oocyte typically occurs in the ampulla, the long, wide portion of the uterine tube. — Slides 45, 51 (Uterine Tubes)"},{"q":"The vagina is positioned posterior to the urethra and anterior to the:","choices":["Rectum","Bladder","Uterus","Sacrum"],"answer":0,"exp":"The vagina is positioned posterior to the urethra and anterior to the rectum. — Slide 57 (Vagina)"},{"q":"The uterine (fallopian) tubes attach to the uterus but characteristically:","choices":["Physically fuse to the ovarian cortex","Attach directly to the urinary bladder wall","Do NOT physically attach to the ovary","Attach firmly to the sacral promontory"],"answer":2,"exp":"The uterine tubes attach to the uterus but characteristically do not physically attach to the ovary; the fimbriae simply sweep the ovulated oocyte into the tube. — Slides 49–50 (Uterine Tubes)"},{"q":"The clitoris, located where the labia minora meet anteriorly, is homologous to which male structure?","choices":["The scrotum","The bulbo-urethral gland","The prostate","The penis"],"answer":3,"exp":"The clitoris, located where the labia minora meet anteriorly, contains erectile tissue and is homologous to the penis. — Slide 71 (External Genitalia)"},{"q":"The greater vestibular (Bartholin's) glands, which secrete mucus into the vestibule, are homologous to which male glands?","choices":["The male prostate gland","Paired seminal vesicle glands","Bulbo-urethral (Cowper's) glands","Preputial (foreskin) glands"],"answer":2,"exp":"The greater vestibular (Bartholin's) glands, which secrete mucus into the vestibule, are homologous to the male bulbo-urethral (Cowper's) glands. — Slide 78 (Greater Vestibular Gland)"},{"q":"The uterine (fallopian) tube is lined by which epithelium that helps move the oocyte toward the uterus?","choices":["Simple squamous endothelial-type epithelium","Transitional epithelium lining the ureters","Ciliated (and non-ciliated) simple columnar epithelium","Stratified squamous epithelium of the vagina"],"answer":2,"exp":"The uterine tube is lined by ciliated and non-ciliated simple columnar epithelium; ciliary beating combined with peristalsis moves the oocyte toward the uterus. — Slides 45, 53 (Uterine Tube histology)"},{"q":"The three tunics of the uterine wall, from outer to inner, are the perimetrium, myometrium, and:","choices":["Adventitia","Endometrium","Serosa","Mucosa propria"],"answer":1,"exp":"The three tunics of the uterine wall, from outer to inner, are the perimetrium, myometrium, and endometrium. — Slides 35, 39 (Uterine Layers)"},{"q":"The myometrium of the uterus is composed primarily of:","choices":["Skeletal muscle","Smooth muscle","Cardiac muscle","Dense regular connective tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"The myometrium is composed primarily of smooth muscle arranged in intertwining layers that hypertrophy and distend markedly during pregnancy. — Slides 35, 39 (Uterine Wall)"},{"q":"The endometrium has two layers; which layer builds up and sheds each month in response to ovarian hormones?","choices":["Stratum basalis layer","Myometrium muscle layer","Perimetrium serosal layer","Stratum functionalis"],"answer":3,"exp":"The stratum functionalis is the endometrial layer that builds up under hormonal stimulation each cycle and sheds during menstruation; the stratum basalis beneath it persists and regenerates the functionalis. — Slide 43 (Endometrium)"},{"q":"The vaginal wall consists of three tunics: an inner mucosa, a middle muscularis, and an outer:","choices":["Serosa","Perimetrium","Adventitia","Submucosa"],"answer":2,"exp":"The vaginal wall consists of an inner mucosa, a middle muscularis, and an outer adventitia. — Slide 56 (Vagina)"},{"q":"An ovarian follicle consists of an oocyte surrounded by:","choices":["Chromaffin cells","Podocytes","Follicular cells","Chief cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"An ovarian follicle consists of an oocyte surrounded by follicular (granulosa) cells. — Slides 25–26 (Ovaries follicle structure)"},{"q":"At menstruation, the functional layer self-digests as the spiral arteries kink and constrict; just before flow, the spiral arteries open wide and:","choices":["Capillaries burst, sloughing the functional layer","The basalis layer degenerates and sheds","The myometrium contracts and remains permanently shortened","The perimetrium thickens and becomes fibrotic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Just before menstrual flow, the spiral arteries dilate, capillaries within the functional layer burst, and the functional layer sloughs off as menstrual blood. — Slide 43 (Endometrium)"},{"q":"Peritoneum folding around the pelvic organs creates the anterior vesicouterine pouch between the uterus and the:","choices":["Rectal wall","Sacral curve","Urinary bladder","Sigmoid colon loop"],"answer":2,"exp":"The anterior vesicouterine pouch is the peritoneal recess between the uterus and the urinary bladder. — Slide 12 (peritoneal pouches)"},{"q":"The posterior rectouterine pouch (of Douglas) forms the space between the uterus anteriorly and the:","choices":["Bladder","Vagina","Rectum","Ovary"],"answer":2,"exp":"The posterior rectouterine pouch (pouch of Douglas) lies between the uterus anteriorly and the rectum posteriorly, and is the most dependent point of the peritoneal cavity in females. — Slide 12 (peritoneal pouches)"},{"q":"The outer serosal covering of most of the uterus, continuous with the broad ligament, is the:","choices":["Adventitia","Myometrium","Endometrium","Perimetrium"],"answer":3,"exp":"The perimetrium is the outer serosal covering of most of the uterus and is continuous with the peritoneum of the broad ligament. — Slide 39 (Wall of the Uterus)"},{"q":"With respect to the peritoneum, the uterus is best described as:","choices":["Intraperitoneal","Retroperitoneal","Subperitoneal only","Completely extraperitoneal"],"answer":0,"exp":"The uterus is intraperitoneal, draped by the peritoneal fold of the broad ligament. — Slide 31 (Uterus)"},{"q":"The ovary lies in which relationship to the broad ligament?","choices":["Anterior to the broad ligament, free within the peritoneal cavity","Inferior to the vaginal fornix and perineal body","Embedded within the myometrium of the uterine wall","Posterior to the broad ligament (via the mesovarium)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The ovary lies posterior to the broad ligament, attached to its posterior layer by the mesovarium. — Slide 24 (Ovary)"},{"q":"The boundaries of the female perineum include the mons pubis, the medial aspects of the thighs, and the gluteal folds; the tip of which bone is palpable at the superior end of the intergluteal cleft?","choices":["Ischium","Coccyx","Pubis","Sacral promontory"],"answer":1,"exp":"The tip of the coccyx is palpable at the superior end of the intergluteal cleft, marking the posterior perineal boundary. — Slide 8 (Boundaries of Female Perineum)"},{"q":"The vestibule of the female external genitalia is the region enclosed by the labia minora that contains the openings of the urethra, the vagina, and the:","choices":["The anal canal and sphincter","Bilateral ureteral openings","Vestibular (Bartholin's) glands","Paired ejaculatory duct openings"],"answer":2,"exp":"The vestibule, enclosed by the labia minora, contains the openings of the urethra, the vagina, and the vestibular (Bartholin's) glands. — Slide 70 (External Genitalia)"},{"q":"During defecation, the levator ani of the pelvic diaphragm acts to:","choices":["Permanently close the anus","Lift to help release feces","Produce peristalsis in the colon","Secrete mucus"],"answer":1,"exp":"During defecation, the levator ani lifts to help release feces, in addition to its ongoing role supporting the pelvic organs. — Slide 9 (Pelvic Diaphragm)"},{"q":"Lymphatic drainage of the ovaries follows the ovarian blood vessels to ascend to which nodes?","choices":["Superficial inguinal lymph nodes","Popliteal fossa lymph nodes","Internal iliac lymph nodes only","Lumbar (para-aortic) lymph nodes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Lymphatic drainage of the ovaries follows the ovarian blood vessels to ascend to the lumbar (para-aortic) lymph nodes, the same nodes that drain the uterine tube. — Slides 27, 48 (ovarian/tubal lymphatics)"},{"q":"The fimbriae are finger-like projections located at which part of the uterine tube?","choices":["The isthmus","The infundibulum","The intramural segment","The ampulla"],"answer":1,"exp":"The fimbriae are finger-like projections located at the infundibulum, the funnel-shaped distal end of the uterine tube nearest the ovary. — Slide 51 (Uterine Tube)"},{"q":"The perimetrium, the outer uterine tunic, is specifically a:","choices":["Mucosa lined by columnar epithelium","Muscularis of interlacing smooth muscle","Serosa (peritoneum with thin connective tissue)","Submucosa of loose connective tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"The perimetrium, the outer uterine tunic, is specifically a serosa — visceral peritoneum backed by a thin layer of connective tissue — continuous with the broad ligament. — Slide 35 (Uterine Layers)"},{"q":"The broad ligament extends from the lateral uterine fold to which structure?","choices":["The sacral promontory","The urinary bladder wall","The labia majora skin","The wall of the pelvis"],"answer":3,"exp":"The broad ligament extends from the lateral uterine fold to the wall of the pelvis, and also encloses/supports the ovaries and uterine tubes. — Slide 18 (Supporting Ligaments)"}]};
